+# Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays a Non-Goal; Attribute Filtering Does Not
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian, on the grounds
+that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once and repeating
+it elsewhere adds surface without adding understanding. A request has since
+arrived to filter the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already
+displays. Filtering a directory by one of its own shown attributes is a
+different capability from free-text search over a name: it teaches restricting
+a cached collection by an attribute, a pattern the sample does not yet show.
+The blanket NG-9 wording swept both together and needs narrowing so the
+distinction is on the record rather than inferred.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 as written** - Treat any veterinarian lookup as out of scope,
+   forcing the filter request to contradict a standing non-goal.
+2. **Retire NG-9 entirely** - Admit veterinarian search of every kind,
+   including the free-text name search the original reason still rejects.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** - Keep free-text veterinarian search out of scope; admit
+   filtering the directory by an attribute it already shows.
+
+## Decision
+
+Narrow NG-9. Free-text veterinarian search stays out of scope. Filtering the
+veterinarian directory by a specialty it already presents is in scope, realized
+by REQ-VET-003.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The distinction between free-text search and attribute filtering is now
+explicit, so a later free-text request is judged against a standing non-goal
+rather than an ambiguous one. The narrowing admits exactly one new pattern —
+attribute filtering of a cached collection — without opening general
+veterinarian search. The original NG-9 reason continues to reject the
+free-text case.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md#req-vet-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003)
