+# Filter the Veterinarian Directory in Memory Over the Cached Collection
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+REQ-VET-003 filters the veterinarian directory by a whole specialty name,
+case-insensitively, on both the HTML page and the serialized `/vets` surface.
+The reflexive realization is a Spring Data query on `VetRepository`
+(`findBySpecialtiesNameIgnoreCase`), matching the Repository pattern the
+codebase uses everywhere else.
+
+Two facts make that realization the wrong fit here. `Vet.specialties` is an
+`EAGER` `@ManyToMany`: a paged query that joins the specialties collection
+cannot page at the database — Hibernate fetches every matching row and
+paginates in memory, warns, and risks duplicate roots without a `DISTINCT`. So
+the "DB paging" advantage of a repository query evaporates for the filtered
+path. Separately, the whole vet collection is already fully cached
+(`CacheConfiguration`, `VetRepository.findAll`) because the directory is small
+and read-only; a per-specialty query multiplies cache entries or bypasses the
+cache entirely.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Spring Data query per surface** - `findBySpecialtiesNameIgnoreCase(...)`
+   paged and unpaged. Idiomatic, but pages in memory anyway under the eager
+   collection, fragments the cache, and defines the match twice.
+2. **In-memory filter over the cached collection** - read the already-cached
+   collection, filter it with a domain predicate on `Vet`, and re-paginate the
+   HTML surface with `PageImpl`. The serialized surface stays unpaged, as it is
+   today.
+
+## Decision
+
+Take option 2. The case-insensitive whole-name match is a domain predicate on
+the aggregate — `Vet` tests whether it holds a named specialty — so both
+surfaces share one definition of matching and no business rule lands in the
+controller. `VetController` reads the cached collection, filters by the
+predicate, and assembles the page for the HTML surface; the serialized surface
+filters the same collection and wraps it in `Vets`. No repository query is
+added. A blank or whitespace-only value is normalized to no filter at the
+controller boundary, mirroring owner search.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The single cached collection is reused, so no per-specialty cache entries
+appear. Both surfaces match identically because one predicate defines it.
+Case folding happens in Java, independent of database collation, so the filter
+sidesteps the vendor case-sensitivity defect class that owner search carries.
+The matching rule lives on `Vet`, keeping the *Web controller* row of the
+pattern catalog satisfied. Negatively, the filtered HTML path paginates in
+memory rather than at the database, and `VetController` gains page-assembly
+orchestration — accepted because the collection is small and cached by design.
+This departs from the Repository pattern for the filter query only; the
+repository remains the sole data source.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts)
+- [prd.md#req-vet-003](../prd.md#req-vet-003)
+- [ADR: Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays a Non-Goal; Attribute Filtering Does Not](2026-08-07-non-goal-free-text-vet-search.md)
