+**Veterinarian directory filtering.** Both vet surfaces accept an optional specialty filter query parameter (REQ-VET-003). When it is present and non-blank, the directory is filtered in memory over the cached vet collection rather than by a repository query: `Vet` tests whole-name, case-insensitive specialty membership, `VetController` filters and re-paginates the HTML surface and filters the serialized collection, and the HTML pagination links carry the filter value so filtered pages stay navigable. A blank or whitespace-only value is treated as no filter, and an empty filter value rendered by a pagination link is inert for the same reason. `VetRepository` is unchanged — the filter reads the cached vet collection. See [ADR: Filter the Veterinarian Directory in Memory Over the Cached Collection](adr/2026-08-07-vet-directory-filter-in-memory.md).
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
